  "question": {
    "text": "If the sum of the series $1^2+2 \\cdot 2^2+3^2+2 \\cdot 4^2+5^2+$ ... $2.6^2+\\ldots$ upto $\\mathrm{n}$ terms, when $\\mathrm{n}$ is even, is $\\frac{n(n+1)^2}{2}$, then the sum of the series, when $\\mathrm{n}$ is odd, is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "GS0gU",
      "text": "\n$n^2(n+1)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "daULH",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{n^2(n-1)}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uNp2D",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{n^2(n+1)}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "zATMk",
      "text": "\n$n^2(n-1)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If $n$ is odd, the required sum is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& 1^2+2.2^2+3^2+2.4^2+\\ldots . .+2(n-1)^2+n^2 \\\\\n& =\\frac{(n-1)(n-1+1)^2}{2}+n^2 \\quad(\\because n-1 \\text { is even }) \\\\\n& =\\left(\\frac{n-1}{2}+1\\right) n^2=\\frac{n^2(\\mathrm{n}+1)}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
